    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d176">

                <pb facs="0276" n="174" xml:id="pg174"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 2648/22–23.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Fletcher to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1908-02-06"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">February 6, 1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 839.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of my No. 790 of November
                    29, 1907, on the subject of the Revised Mining Regulations for China, I have the
                    honor to inclose copy of a note which I have to-day addressed to the Wai-wu Pu
                    in accordance with the department’s telegraphic instructions of the 3d instant,
                        reading:<anchor n="1" xml:id="d176-f1"/></p>
                <p>Up to this time the Chinese Government has made no reply to the British
                    minister’s note and I have asked the foreign office for a reply to my note to
                    the effect that the regulations will be thoroughly revised and modified in the
                    light of the objections of the foreign representatives before being put into
                    effect.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry P.
                    Fletcher.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d176-a1.xml" xpointer="d176-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
